# A shop counter or a shelf run: one tile square, waist high, flat topped.
#
# Y is up, +Z is north, one world unit is one map tile. The lowest vertex sits
# on y = 0 and the model is centred on x = 0 and z = 0, so the game places it
# by its feet.
#
# Faces are wound so that (c - a) x (b - a) points out of the solid, which is
# what tools/obj2cpp.py bakes as the face normal. Get it backwards and the
# model still draws, it just goes flat ambient dark.
#
# Four faces, 8 triangles. Same box as wall.obj at 0.5 units, and half a wall
# on purpose: the clerk stands behind it and has to be visible over it, which
# is why this is not wall.obj at a smaller scale. Scaling a mesh shrinks its
# footprint too, and that would leave a gap of floor between one counter tile
# and the next.

mtllib counter.mtl

v -0.52 0.00 -0.52
v  0.52 0.00 -0.52
v  0.52 0.00  0.52
v -0.52 0.00  0.52
v -0.52 0.50 -0.52
v  0.52 0.50 -0.52
v  0.52 0.50  0.52
v -0.52 0.50  0.52

usemtl counter_top
f 5 6 7 8

usemtl counter_face
f 1 2 6 5

usemtl counter_side
f 2 3 7 6
f 4 1 5 8
